    },
    {
      "id": "Y3uX1YrTA_LcTOvlajtui",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 11999.202902020028,
      "y": 12497.027455348461,
      "width": 414.3157653808594,
      "height": 105,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 504767859,
      "version": 94,
      "versionNonce": 1119187325,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783311971285,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- inverted index for full text\n- geospatial index for location\n- index on category",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- inverted index for full text\n- geospatial index for location\n- index on category",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Fy5L9tz94tR6HllIczawo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10644.345167572304,
      "y": 13487.618371816056,
      "width": 1910.49462890625,
      "height": 840,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 122195517,
      "version": 241,
      "versionNonce": 2092353235,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783312769409,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Precomputed Location Tags (vs. Bounding Box Search)\nProblem with bounding boxes: Computing/filtering by bounding box on every read request is wasted work — the geometry of \"which areas \ndoes this business belong to\" almost never changes, but we recompute it on every query.\n\nIdea: Shift the geo computation from read time to write time.\n\nWhen a business is created (or its location changes), resolve its lat/lng against a predefined hierarchy of areas \n(region → city → neighborhood) and store the result as a flat list of location tags — e.g. location_names: \n[\"bay_area\", \"san_francisco\", \"mission_district\"].\n\nThese tags are just strings/enums, no coordinates involved.\nA business can belong to multiple overlapping areas at once (city + neighborhood + region), which naturally handles boundary cases.\n\nIndexing: Store location_names as a keyword field in Elasticsearch. This gives you a plain inverted index — each area name has a postings \nlist of business IDs, just like a tag or category field.\n\nQuery time: Searching \"restaurants in San Francisco\" becomes a simple term filter on location_names, run in filter context \n(not query/scoring context) so Elasticsearch caches it as a bitset. No geometry, no per-request computation — just a term lookup.\n\nCost shift: Expensive point-in-polygon resolution happens once per business write, not once per search request. Since reads vastly \noutnumber writes for most business/local-search systems, this is a big net win.\n\nTrade-offs:\nTakeaway: Precompute what's stable",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Precomputed Location Tags (vs. Bounding Box Search)\nProblem with bounding boxes: Computing/filtering by bounding box on every read request is wasted work — the geometry of \"which areas \ndoes this business belong to\" almost never changes, but we recompute it on every query.\n\nIdea: Shift the geo computation from read time to write time.\n\nWhen a business is created (or its location changes), resolve its lat/lng against a predefined hierarchy of areas \n(region → city → neighborhood) and store the result as a flat list of location tags — e.g. location_names: \n[\"bay_area\", \"san_francisco\", \"mission_district\"].\n\nThese tags are just strings/enums, no coordinates involved.\nA business can belong to multiple overlapping areas at once (city + neighborhood + region), which naturally handles boundary cases.\n\nIndexing: Store location_names as a keyword field in Elasticsearch. This gives you a plain inverted index — each area name has a postings \nlist of business IDs, just like a tag or category field.\n\nQuery time: Searching \"restaurants in San Francisco\" becomes a simple term filter on location_names, run in filter context \n(not query/scoring context) so Elasticsearch caches it as a bitset. No geometry, no per-request computation — just a term lookup.\n\nCost shift: Expensive point-in-polygon resolution happens once per business write, not once per search request. Since reads vastly \noutnumber writes for most business/local-search systems, this is a big net win.\n\nTrade-offs:\nTakeaway: Precompute what's stable",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
